<BaseLayout
  {title}
  {description}
  canonical="https://divorceu.ae/en/talaq-uae/"
  {hreflang}
  lang="en"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-4">
    <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} />
  </div>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero-section">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <p class="hero-tag">Talaq in UAE, 2025 Law Guide</p>
      <h1>Talaq in UAE: All Types, Court Rules and Your Rights</h1>
      <p class="hero-desc">
        Under Article 57 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, saying talaq three times at once counts as one talaq, not three. The husband must document a talaq at court within 15 days, but missing that deadline does not cancel it: it makes him liable to compensate his wife for the delay. Here is what UAE law actually says, what your rights are, and what happens step by step.
      </p>
      <p class="hero-author">By <strong>Aleksi Suon</strong>, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant &middot; Updated October 2026</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="page-layout">
    <main class="main-content">

      <!-- Key facts box -->
      <div class="answer-box">
        <h2>Six things you need to know right now</h2>
        <ul class="key-facts">
          <li><strong>Triple talaq at once = ONE talaq in UAE.</strong> Article 57 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 counts a repeated divorce as one. A first or second talaq is revocable during the iddah.</li>
          <li><strong>The husband must document the talaq at court within 15 days.</strong> Article 58. This is a duty, not a validity condition: if he is late without an accepted excuse, the wife gets compensation equal to maintenance for the delay.</li>
          <li><strong>WhatsApp talaq can be valid</strong>, because Article 57 recognises a written divorce. It still has to be documented within 15 days, and until it is documented or proven in court there is no court record to rely on.</li>
          <li><strong>The wife's financial rights depend on the type of divorce:</strong> iddah housing in every case, iddah maintenance after a revocable talaq, the dowry on irrevocable separation, mut'a where the husband divorced by his own will (Articles 46, 101 and 102).</li>
          <li><strong>Non-Muslims cannot use talaq.</strong> A separate civil divorce track applies under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022.</li>
          <li><strong>Revocation (raj'i) is possible during iddah</strong> without a new nikah, for revocable talaq types.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 1: What Is Talaq -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>What Is Talaq and Who Does It Apply To?</h2>
        <p>
          Talaq is the Islamic mechanism by which a husband dissolves a marriage by pronouncement. It is rooted in Quranic guidance (Surah Al-Talaq, 65:1-7) and classical fiqh, and it is given legal force in the UAE by <strong>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024</strong> on Personal Status (in force 15 April 2025), which replaced the former Federal Law No. 28 of 2005.
        </p>
        <p>
          Talaq applies exclusively to Muslim marriages. If you are a non-Muslim resident of the UAE, see the <a href="/en/types-of-divorce-uae/">types of divorce in UAE guide</a> for the civil divorce track under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022. Non-Muslims have no talaq mechanism, they file for no-fault civil dissolution at the Personal Status Court using a separate procedure.
        </p>
        <p>
          UAE law does not simply defer to whatever form of talaq was pronounced. The statute sets its own rules: Article 56 lists cases where a talaq does not occur at all (for example under duress, or in anger so intense that it prevents control of one's words), Article 57 counts a repeated divorce as one, and Article 58 requires the husband to document the talaq at court within 15 days, with compensation to the wife if he delays without an acceptable excuse. These rules apply to Muslim residents including expatriates from Pakistan, India, Egypt, Jordan, and the wider Arab world. Home-country recognition is its own step: see our guides for <a href="/en/pakistani-divorce-uae/">Pakistani nationals</a> (NADRA registration and the Union Council notice) and <a href="/en/indian-national-divorce-uae/">Indian nationals</a>.
        </p>
        <div class="info-box info-box--info">
          <h3>Which courts handle talaq in UAE?</h3>
          <p>Personal Status Courts in each emirate. In Dubai: the Dubai Personal Status Court at the Dubai Courts complex. In Abu Dhabi: the Abu Dhabi Family Court. In Sharjah, Ajman, Ras Al Khaimah, Umm Al Quwain, and Fujairah: emirate-level Personal Status Courts. All operate under the same federal law framework.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: The Three Types -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The Three Types of Talaq: What UAE Courts Actually Apply</h2>
        <p>
          Classical Islamic jurisprudence identifies two to three categories of talaq depending on the school of thought. UAE courts apply the following framework in practice.
        </p>

        <div class="type-grid">
          {talaqTypes.map((t) => (
            <div class={`type-card type-card--${t.flag}`}>
              <div class="type-header">
                <span class={`type-badge type-badge--${t.flag}`}>{t.type}</span>
                <span class="type-meaning">{t.meaning}</span>
              </div>
              <div class="type-body">
                <p class="type-how"><strong>How it is done:</strong> {t.how}</p>
                <p class="type-rev"><strong>Revocable:</strong> {t.revocable}</p>
                <p class="type-detail">{t.detail}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h3>The triple talaq question, what UAE courts actually do</h3>
        <p>
          This is one of the most misunderstood points in UAE family law. When a husband says "talaq talaq talaq" three times in a single session, many people, and many foreign lawyers, assume the divorce is immediately and irrevocably final. This was the position of the majority of classical jurists in the four Sunni schools, and it is the approach that several South Asian jurisdictions historically followed (India, Bangladesh, and Pakistan have all legislated against it).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The UAE does not follow this position.</strong> Article 57 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 provides that a repeated divorce, or a divorce coupled with a number, made verbally, in writing or by signal, counts as ONE divorce. The one-count view is associated with Ibn Taymiyya and Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya, who drew on a hadith narrated in the Musnad of Ahmad ibn Hanbal; the UAE has put it into the statute. The practical effect: if your husband says "I divorce you, I divorce you, I divorce you" in one conversation, by text, voice, or in person, that is one talaq. If it is his first or second talaq and the marriage was consummated, you are in a revocable divorce, with the iddah period ahead of you.
        </p>
        <p>
          A major irrevocable divorce is reached only by the talaq that completes three (Article 59(2)(b)). Those three can fall within a single marriage: after a first or second talaq the husband can take his wife back during the iddah (Article 61), and that talaq still counts towards the three. If the iddah ends without a take back, the first or second talaq becomes minor irrevocable and the couple can reunite only with a new contract and a new dowry (Article 59(2)(a)). What does not produce a major irrevocable divorce is three words in one sitting, because Article 57 counts them as one.
        </p>
        <div class="info-box info-box--warning">
          <h3>Why this matters practically</h3>
          <p>If you received a "triple talaq" by message or in person, you may have been told the divorce is final and you are free to remarry. Under UAE law, you are not, at least not yet. Article 57 counts it as one talaq; if it is a first or second talaq the husband can take you back during the iddah, and a woman may not marry until her iddah ends (Article 83). In practice a new marriage also needs a court divorce document. Acting on an undocumented triple talaq, including attempting to remarry, creates serious legal complications. Consult a UAE lawyer before taking any steps.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Article 58 Registration -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The 15-Day Court Registration Rule (Article 58, FL 41/2024)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Article 58 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024</strong> is the provision that governs putting a talaq on the court record. It provides that the husband shall document the divorce before the competent court within a maximum of 15 days from its occurrence, and that this does not prejudice the wife's right to file a case to prove the divorce (Article 58(1)). If he does not document it within 15 days without an excuse acceptable to the court, the wife is entitled to compensation equal to maintenance from the date of the divorce to the date of documentation (Article 58(2)).
        </p>
        <p>
          Note what the article does not say. It does not make documentation a condition for the talaq to exist, and it does not say an undocumented talaq has no effect. The deadline is a duty backed by a financial penalty. In practice, though, an undocumented talaq is hard to rely on: courts and the authorities work from the court record, and where a divorce is proven in court later, the iddah runs from when it is proven by evidence or admission, with the divorce dated to the admission unless an earlier date is proven (Article 83).
        </p>
        <div class="info-box info-box--info">
          <h3>Reading Article 58 correctly</h3>
          <p>The 15-day duty and its article number are confirmed in the official text of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, and durations in the law run by the Gregorian calendar unless stated otherwise (Article 2). The safe practice is simple: the husband documents promptly, and a wife whose husband has not done so files her own case to prove the divorce rather than waiting.</p>
        </div>

        <h3>What the registration process looks like</h3>
        <div class="steps-list">
          <div class="step-card">
            <div class="step-num">01</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>File at the Personal Status Court</h3>
              <p>The husband (or his authorised representative with a notarised power of attorney) files to document the talaq. Documents required: passport, Emirates ID, original marriage certificate (attested and Arabic-translated for foreign marriages), and the court filing fee (check the current amount on the court's service page).</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="step-card">
            <div class="step-num">02</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Family Guidance, only if the judge refers it</h3>
              <p>Under Article 8 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, the supervising judge may refer the spouses to Family Guidance (Idarat al-Irshad al-Usari) if he or she thinks an amicable settlement is feasible. Referral is not automatic, and cases to prove a divorce are expressly exempt. There is no statutory time limit for the stage. Where spouses do settle matters such as maintenance or custody there, the settlement is recorded in a report signed by them and the counsellor, approved by the judge, and has the force of an execution writ. Family Guidance does not undo a talaq that has been given: only the husband can take his wife back, during the iddah of a revocable talaq (Article 61).</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="step-card">
            <div class="step-num">03</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Court hearing and talaq certificate</h3>
              <p>Where there was a Family Guidance referral, the file returns to the court afterwards. If the talaq is uncontested, the judge issues the divorce certificate (Certificate of Talaq) at or shortly after the hearing. The certificate is signed by the judge and carries the court seal. This is the document that legally records the divorce in UAE civil records.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="step-card">
            <div class="step-num">04</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Registration in civil records</h3>
              <p>The court registers the divorce in the civil status system. For non-UAE nationals, the relevant embassy or consulate is typically notified. For residents with UAE National ID cards or family books, the personal status register is updated. The divorce certificate can then be attested for use abroad.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>What if the husband refuses to register?</h3>
        <p>
          If a husband pronounced talaq verbally or in writing but refuses or fails to document it within 15 days, the wife has two routes. First, she can file a case to prove the divorce, a right Article 58 expressly preserves, submitting evidence of the pronouncement (witnesses, messages, audio). The court examines the evidence and, if satisfied, confirms the divorce without the husband's active cooperation, and she can claim compensation equal to maintenance for the period of his delay. A case to prove a divorce is exempt from Family Guidance referral (Article 8).
        </p>
        <p>
          Second: the wife can file for judicial divorce (faskh) on grounds such as harm, failure to maintain, desertion or absence under Articles 71 to 79 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024. This is a woman-initiated dissolution that does not depend on proving the talaq was pronounced, instead it proceeds on independent grounds. See the <a href="/en/khula-divorce-uae/">khula divorce guide</a> for woman-initiated options and the <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-dubai/">step-by-step filing guide</a> for the full process.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Digital Talaq -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Digital Talaq: WhatsApp, SMS, Email, and Voice Notes</h2>
        <p>
          One of the most frequently asked questions in UAE family law is whether a divorce sent by WhatsApp or text message is valid. The short answer: it can be, but it does not automatically end the marriage.
        </p>
        <p>
          The legal basis for digital talaq is the personal status law itself. <strong>Article 57 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024</strong> recognises a divorce made verbally, in writing or by signal, so a written message can carry a talaq. A WhatsApp message saying "I divorce you" with clear intent, sent by a husband to his wife, can amount to one. Whether a particular message counts is a question of intent and evidence, and Article 56 lists the cases in which a talaq does not occur at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          However, the message is only the first step. The husband must still document the talaq at court within 15 days under Article 58. Until it is documented or proven in court there is no official record: the wife cannot remarry on the strength of the message, and a husband who leaves it undocumented without an acceptable excuse owes her compensation equal to maintenance for the delay. He cannot use an undocumented message to walk away from his obligations.
        </p>
        <p>
          Witnesses are not a condition for a talaq in the four Sunni schools, so a WhatsApp message sent to the wife alone, without any witnesses, can constitute a valid pronouncement. What matters is whether the court accepts the evidence of the pronouncement when the divorce is documented or proven.
        </p>
        <p>
          For more detail on the legal status of digital divorces and what to do if you received one, see the dedicated <a href="/en/whatsapp-divorce-uae/">WhatsApp divorce UAE guide</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: Iddah and Revocation -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The Iddah Period: What Happens After a Talaq</h2>
        <p>
          The iddah (waiting period) is the period that follows a talaq. After a revocable talaq, the divorce is not yet final during the iddah: the marriage contract continues until it expires (Article 59), and reconciliation remains possible.
        </p>

        <h3>Duration of iddah</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Non-pregnant woman:</strong> Three periods of purity (quru', tuhr) if she menstruates. Three lunar months if she is post-menopausal or has never menstruated (Article 84).</li>
          <li><strong>Pregnant woman:</strong> Iddah lasts until she gives birth or miscarries, however long that takes (Article 85).</li>
          <li><strong>Divorce before consummation:</strong> No iddah applies only if there was neither intercourse nor valid seclusion (khalwa) in the marriage (Articles 17 and 82). Such a divorce is minor irrevocable (Article 60), and she is entitled to half the named dowry (Article 47(3)). On the husband's death an iddah applies even before intercourse (Article 82).</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Wife's rights during iddah</h3>
        <p>
          After a revocable talaq, the husband must maintain his wife during the iddah: housing, food, clothing, and medical expenses (Article 101). After an irrevocable divorce, iddah maintenance is due only if she is pregnant, until the birth, and there is none after khula. In every case she may live in the marital home during the iddah, or in a suitable home if the marital home is unsuitable.
        </p>

        <h3>Revocation (Raj'i) during iddah</h3>
        <p>
          For a revocable talaq (Talaq Ahsan or first/second pronouncement of Talaq Hasan), the husband can take his wife back during the iddah by an explicit spoken or written statement or by action (Articles 61 and 62). No new nikah contract is required, and the wife's consent is not required, but the 2024 law requires him to inform her immediately and, if he documented the divorce, to document the take back at court within 15 days (Article 64).
        </p>
        <p>
          After the iddah period ends without a take back, the divorce becomes minor irrevocable (ba'in sughra). To remarry each other at this point, the parties need a new contract and a new dowry (Article 59). The talaq that completes three produces a major irrevocable divorce (ba'in kubra): she is not permitted to him until she has married another man in a valid marriage, that husband actually consummated it, and her iddah from him has ended (Article 59(2)(b)). A pre-arranged "tahlil" marriage made only so that she can return to the first husband is widely condemned by Islamic scholars.
        </p>

        <h3>How a revocation is recorded at court</h3>
        <p>
          The practical point that trips up many couples is timing. A take back only works while the iddah is still running (Article 61), so the calendar matters more than the words. Article 64 sets the formalities: the husband must inform his wife of the take back immediately, even if the divorce was never documented, and if he documented the divorce he must document the take back before the competent court within 15 days. If he does not document it in time and she, not knowing of it, marries another man, the take back is invalid. Once a valid take back is in place, the marriage simply continues, with no new dower and no new contract.
        </p>
        <p>
          A revocation that is never documented is the source of most later disputes. A husband may claim he revoked in week two; the wife may say she was never told and treated herself as divorced. Where the pronouncement was already recorded at court, an undocumented revocation is difficult to prove after the fact, and a court will look closely at whether the parties actually resumed married life. The counting of the iddah itself decides how much time is left to act, and it runs differently for a pregnant wife, a menstruating wife, and a post-menopausal wife. See the <a href="/en/iddah-waiting-period-uae/">iddah waiting period guide</a> for how each variant is measured and when the window to revoke closes.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6: Financial Rights -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The Wife's Financial Rights After Talaq</h2>
        <p>
          After a talaq the wife has a set of financial rights. Which ones apply, and when they fall due, depends on the type of divorce. These are not negotiable gifts, they are legally enforceable debts.
        </p>

        <div class="rights-grid">
          <div class="right-card">
            <div class="right-icon">M</div>
            <div class="right-body">
              <h3>Mahr (Dower)</h3>
              <p>The dowry, including any deferred portion (mu'ajjal), falls due on irrevocable separation (Article 46): when the talaq is irrevocable, or when the iddah of a revocable talaq ends without a take back. If the divorce came before intercourse or valid seclusion, she is entitled to half the named dowry (Article 47(3)). It is enforceable as a privileged debt: if the husband refuses to pay, the wife can obtain a court order and proceed to the Execution Court for bank freeze, salary garnishment, or travel ban. See <a href="/en/mahr-divorce-uae/">mahr in UAE divorce</a> for enforcement steps.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="right-card">
            <div class="right-icon">I</div>
            <div class="right-body">
              <h3>Iddah Maintenance (Nafaqat al-Idda)</h3>
              <p>Housing, food, clothing, and medical support for the iddah after a revocable talaq. After an irrevocable divorce it is due only if she is pregnant, until the birth, and there is none after khula or death. In every case she may live in the marital home during the iddah (Article 101). Interim maintenance orders can be requested in urgent cases.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="right-card">
            <div class="right-icon">T</div>
            <div class="right-body">
              <h3>Mut'a (Consolation Payment)</h3>
              <p>Compensation on top of iddah maintenance, owed where the husband divorced a wife with whom the marriage was consummated, by his sole will, without her request or any cause from her (Article 102). The court sets it according to his means, up to one year's maintenance for women of her standing, can allow instalments, and takes into account the harm she suffered.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="right-card">
            <div class="right-icon">N</div>
            <div class="right-body">
              <h3>Child Nafaqa</h3>
              <p>If children are in the mother's custody (hadana), the father is obligated to pay monthly child support. UAE courts set this based on the father's income and the children's needs. The obligation continues until boys reach financial independence and girls marry.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="right-card">
            <div class="right-icon">H</div>
            <div class="right-body">
              <h3>Housing for Children (Sukna)</h3>
              <p>The father must provide housing suitable for the custodial mother and children during the hadana period. This is separate from mahr and iddah maintenance, it is an ongoing obligation for as long as the children are in the mother's custody.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          For a detailed look at the <a href="/en/divorce-cost-uae/">full financial cost of divorce in UAE</a> including court fees, lawyer costs, and typical mahr enforcement timelines, see our dedicated cost guide. For specific information on the <a href="/en/divorce-timeline-uae/">timeline from filing to final certificate</a>, that guide covers each stage.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section: Talaq vs other routes -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Talaq Compared to Khula, Faskh, and Tatliq</h2>
        <p>
          Talaq is only one of the routes out of a Muslim marriage in the UAE, and it is the one route the wife cannot start on her own. Understanding where it sits next to the others tells you whether you even need a talaq at all, or whether a different filing gets you a faster, cleaner result. All four routes run through the same Personal Status Court under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, but they differ on who can start them, whose agreement is needed, and what happens to the mahr.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Talaq</strong> is husband-initiated. The husband pronounces the divorce and must document it at court within 15 days (Article 58); the wife's consent is not a legal requirement.</li>
          <li><strong>Khula</strong> is wife-initiated by agreement to release. The wife asks to be freed from the marriage, usually by returning the mahr or waiving deferred financial claims, though she cannot give up the children's rights (Article 66). If the husband stubbornly refuses, the court can order khula for an appropriate consideration, and it takes effect as a minor irrevocable divorce (Articles 65 and 66). See the <a href="/en/khula-divorce-uae/">khula divorce guide</a> for the mahr-return rules.</li>
          <li><strong>Faskh</strong> is a judicial dissolution the court orders for a defect or fault in the marriage, for example harm, desertion, non-payment of maintenance, imprisonment, or a serious undisclosed illness. It does not depend on the husband pronouncing anything, and in a proven-harm case the wife usually keeps her mahr.</li>
          <li><strong>Tatliq</strong> is the term often used for a court-decreed divorce granted on the wife's petition for grounds such as harm or persistent discord, in some cases through court-appointed arbitrators. Sources and older statutes use faskh and tatliq with overlapping meanings, so treat the label as less important than the ground being argued and confirm the correct filing with the court.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="compare-wrap">
          <table class="compare-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Route</th><th>Who starts it</th><th>Husband's agreement</th><th>Usual effect on mahr</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Talaq</strong></td><td>Husband</td><td>Not needed</td><td>Deferred mahr falls due to the wife</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Khula</strong></td><td>Wife</td><td>Court can proceed without it</td><td>Wife usually returns or waives the mahr</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Faskh</strong></td><td>Wife (court decree)</td><td>Not needed</td><td>Wife usually keeps the mahr on proven fault</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Tatliq</strong></td><td>Wife (court decree)</td><td>Not needed</td><td>Depends on the ground proven</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The practical takeaway for a wife whose husband will not cooperate: you do not have to wait for him to pronounce or register a talaq. A faskh or tatliq petition puts the decision in the court's hands on independent grounds. For the full list of accepted grounds and the evidence each one needs, see the <a href="/en/grounds-for-divorce-uae/">grounds for divorce in UAE guide</a>. If the marriage broke down but there is no fault to prove, khula is usually the quicker path. Repeated pronouncements do not change this map; even a "triple" talaq counts as a single divorce under Article 57, explained in full in the <a href="/en/triple-talaq-uae/">triple talaq guide</a>. And a talaq sent only by message still has to be documented at court within 15 days, covered in the <a href="/en/whatsapp-divorce-uae/">WhatsApp divorce guide</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 7: Talaq in Anger -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Talaq Pronounced in Anger: The UAE Court Position</h2>
        <p>
          A husband who regrets having pronounced talaq sometimes argues that the words were spoken in anger and did not reflect genuine intent. UAE courts address this carefully but the standard for a successful "anger" defence is high.
        </p>
        <p>
          Classical Islamic jurisprudence identifies three states of anger: mild anger (where the person knows what they are saying), moderate anger (where judgment is impaired but the person retains basic awareness), and extreme anger in which the person loses control of what they say. Article 56 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 says a divorce does not occur in anger so intense that it prevents control of one's words. The same article separately excludes a talaq given under duress, or by an insane or intoxicated person, even one intoxicated by choice.
        </p>
        <p>
          The practical bar for the anger exception is high, and it has to be proven with evidence such as witness accounts of what happened at the time. "I was upset" is not enough. Intoxication is treated differently: Article 56 says a talaq by an intoxicated person does not occur even if the intoxication was voluntary, though the husband still has to prove he was intoxicated when he spoke.
        </p>
        <p>
          The more common outcome: a husband who pronounced a first or second talaq in anger and genuinely regrets it should focus on taking his wife back during the iddah, not on challenging the validity of the pronouncement. That is the legally simpler and more reliable path.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 8: Non-Muslim Parallel -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Non-Muslims: No Talaq Mechanism Available</h2>
        <p>
          Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 created a completely separate civil personal status law for non-Muslims in the UAE. Under this law, there is no concept of talaq, khula, mahr, or iddah. Non-Muslim couples file for civil divorce using a no-fault dissolution procedure: there is no reconciliation or Family Guidance stage, and an uncontested divorce can be granted at the first hearing.
        </p>
        <p>
          For information on <a href="/en/types-of-divorce-uae/">all divorce types available in UAE</a>, including civil no-fault divorce, judicial faskh, and court-initiated dissolution, see the overview guide. For non-Muslim expats specifically, the <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-dubai/">how to file guide</a> explains the practical steps at the Personal Status Court.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <FAQSection items={faqItems} />

